  "segments": [
    {
      "segment_id": "S00001",
      "start_char": 0,
      "end_char": 201,
      "text": "MEXICO\nactual prices are expected to broadly converge, drastically reducing subsidies next year. Moreover,\npast experience evidences the authorities’ willingness to remove subsidies in this situation.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00002",
      "start_char": 201,
      "end_char": 395,
      "text": "5. Fuel subsidies have helped to contain inflation in 2022. As global fuel prices rose after\nRussia’s invasion of Ukraine, subsidies substantially contained retail fuel prices. Peaking in June,\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00003",
      "start_char": 395,
      "end_char": 592,
      "text": "subsidies reduced prices by as much as 30 percent. With fuel at about 6 percent of the consumption\nbasket, the direct effects of the fuel subsidy reduced headline inflation by about two percentage\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00004",
      "start_char": 592,
      "end_char": 793,
      "text": "points, when it would have peaked at nearly 10 percent. This estimate of the impact of subsidies on\ninflation may also be a lower bound given second round effects, for example the effect of lower fuel\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00005",
      "start_char": 793,
      "end_char": 1004,
      "text": "prices on retail goods for which distribution is a significant production cost, or public transport costs\nwhere many cities have flexible pricing that responds to fuel prices. However, falling fuel prices since\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00006",
      "start_char": 1004,
      "end_char": 1176,
      "text": "the early summer have reduced the effect of subsidies on inflation, with their effect now estimated\nat a modest 0.2 percent in direct contribution to containing inflation.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00007",
      "start_char": 1176,
      "end_char": 1357,
      "text": "6. Meanwhile, the Mexican government has sought to leverage pre-existing supply-side\npolicies funded by small budget reallocations to tackle food price inflation. Launched in 2019,\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00008",
      "start_char": 1357,
      "end_char": 1545,
      "text": "the Sembrando Vida policy is aimed at addressing rural poverty, tackling environmental degradation,\nand reducing Mexico’s dependence on imported foods by providing financial incentives to\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00009",
      "start_char": 1545,
      "end_char": 1733,
      "text": "introduce more sustainable and higher-yielding farming techniques. Producción para el Bienestar\nfollows a similar model but for small and medium grain producers while price guarantees for\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00010",
      "start_char": 1733,
      "end_char": 1925,
      "text": "producers of some products and delivery of fertilizer to small-scale farmers in specific states have\naimed at providing direct support to the agriculture sector. As the impact of shortages in\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00011",
      "start_char": 1925,
      "end_char": 2125,
      "text": "agricultural inputs have continued to mount, the government has scaled up some of these schemes,\nextending the fertilizer program to nine states from five and also introducing an emergency fertilizer\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00012",
      "start_char": 2125,
      "end_char": 2326,
      "text": "production program. Pre-existing policies aimed at households have also received greater attention\nduring this period of rising food prices, including the Liconsa milk subsidy for households below the\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00013",
      "start_char": 2326,
      "end_char": 2507,
      "text": "extreme poverty line and the provision of subsidized food at Diconsa stores, a network of\ncommunity-owned stores supported by the government. The focus on pre-existing policies has\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00014",
      "start_char": 2507,
      "end_char": 2703,
      "text": "provided the government with instruments to react relatively quickly to price pressures; however,\nthe scale of these policies is somewhat limited at around 0.3 percent of GDP. Moreover, a lack of\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00015",
      "start_char": 2703,
      "end_char": 2898,
      "text": "clarity on policy objectives may serve to hinder efficacy. For example, the objective of Sembrando\nVida appears to be to both reduce rural poverty and reduce inflation by boosting food supplies.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00016",
      "start_char": 2898,
      "end_char": 3086,
      "text": "However, the requirement that recipients demonstrate land ownership likely excludes the poorest\nwhile focusing on smaller farmers may not represent the best way of increasing food supply.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00017",
      "start_char": 3086,
      "end_char": 3258,
      "text": "7. Newer policies have attempted to limit the pass-through of higher prices to\nconsumers directly but with unclear impact. In May, the President announced that a number of\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00018",
      "start_char": 3258,
      "end_char": 3459,
      "text": "major retailers in Mexico had agreed to restrain price increases for 24 staple goods initially for six\nmonths with no fiscal or regulatory implications for the government. Additionally, the government\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00019",
      "start_char": 3459,
      "end_char": 3648,
      "text": "also announced a reduction in tariffs for imports of 21 basic goods and five inputs for food\nproduction. Given domestic production of a number of these goods and the exemption from tariffs\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00020",
      "start_char": 3648,
      "end_char": 3828,
      "text": "of imports under free trade agreements, the cost of this policy in terms of revenues foregone is\nexpected to be small—around MEX$1.2 billion in 2022.\nINTERNATIONAL MONETARY FUND 71"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "schema_version": "locator_index_v1",
