    {
      "segment_id": "S00001",
      "start_char": 0,
      "end_char": 170,
      "text": "MEXICO\nAnnex VII. When the U.S. Sneezes, Does Mexico Catch a Cold?\nThis annex presents estimates of the effect of economic fluctuations in the U.S. on Mexico. As exports\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00002",
      "start_char": 170,
      "end_char": 364,
      "text": "from Mexico to the U.S. rose threefold as a share of GDP since the start of the North America Free\nTrade Agreement (NAFTA), economic growth in Mexico is increasingly sensitive to business cycle\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00003",
      "start_char": 364,
      "end_char": 578,
      "text": "fluctuations in the U.S. At the same time, remittances and capital flows increased as well, but there is\nno correlation between portfolio flows and business fluctuation and remittances rather seem\ncountercyclical.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00004",
      "start_char": 578,
      "end_char": 807,
      "text": "1. Since the start of the Increasing Association between United States and Mexico Growth\nTime-varying effect of unit change in U.S. growth on Mexico in percentage points\nNAFTA in the mid-90s, the\n3\nMexican economy has become 2.5\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00005",
      "start_char": 807,
      "end_char": 1017,
      "text": "increasingly integrated with the 2\n1.5\nU.S. economy. In the\n1\nmanufacturing sector, about half of 0.5\ndomestic value added is now 0\n-0.5\ndriven by foreign demand. In the\n-1\nautomotive industry, the share is 80\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00006",
      "start_char": 1017,
      "end_char": 1139,
      "text": "percent. Most exports are sent to\nSources: U.S. Bureau of Economic Analysis, INEGI; and Fund Staff calculations.\nthe U.S. "
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00007",
      "start_char": 1139,
      "end_char": 1291,
      "text": "At the same time, reported Note: 12-year Rolling window regressionsof Mexico quarterly real GDP growth on United States contemporaneous and lagged real\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00008",
      "start_char": 1291,
      "end_char": 1418,
      "text": "GDP growth. The solid line is the sum of contemporaneous and lagged coefficients and the dashed line the 90 percent confidence\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00009",
      "start_char": 1418,
      "end_char": 1658,
      "text": "interval (Newey-West estimator to allow serial correlation). For instance, 2008-2019 estimates indicate that a 1 percent lower growth\nremittances sent by Mexican\nin the United States is associated with a 1.5 percent lower growth in Mexico.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00010",
      "start_char": 1658,
      "end_char": 1890,
      "text": "workers located in the U.S. have\nincreased steadily from slightly less than 1 percent of GDP in 2000 to almost 4 percent of GDP in\n2021. The Mexican economy has also attracted foreign investors, with gross international liabilities\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00011",
      "start_char": 1890,
      "end_char": 2067,
      "text": "increasing from about 50 percent of GDP in 2001 to about 100 percent of GDP in 2021.\n2. As a result of this rising integration, the Mexican business cycle has increasingly been\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00012",
      "start_char": 2067,
      "end_char": 2263,
      "text": "associated with that of the U.S. A regression analysis with a rolling time window illustrates this\npattern. A one-percent change in U.S. GDP was associated with a 1.5 percent change in Mexico GDP\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00013",
      "start_char": 2263,
      "end_char": 2453,
      "text": "in the 2010s, compared to about 0.2 percent in the 1980s (Figure above). Much of the increase\nseems to reflect the trade channel. Even though the manufacturing sector accounts for only one-\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00014",
      "start_char": 2453,
      "end_char": 2648,
      "text": "fifth of GDP, it explains about three-fourths of Mexico’s short-term GDP fluctuations. Production in\nthis sector is much more reactive to short-term developments than production elsewhere in the\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00015",
      "start_char": 2648,
      "end_char": 2840,
      "text": "economy. In fact, there is a one-to-one short-term association between Mexico’s manufacturing\nexports and production in the U.S. The relative greater importance of the manufacturing sector in\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00016",
      "start_char": 2840,
      "end_char": 3035,
      "text": "Mexico compared to the U.S. (almost one-fifth of GDP versus about one-tenth in the U.S.) could\nexplain why U.S. growth fluctuations could lead to relatively larger growth fluctuations in Mexico.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00017",
      "start_char": 3035,
      "end_char": 3268,
      "text": "Furthermore, during the post-pandemic recovery, Mexico benefited from a shift in U.S. consumption\nfrom services to goods, but this may be an additional headwind in the coming year, as the demand\ncomposition is expected to normalize.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00018",
      "start_char": 3268,
      "end_char": 3447,
      "text": "3. By contrast, remittances seem to be countercyclical; they were increasing in both the\nGFC and the COVID-19 pandemic.1 During the GFC, the increase partly reflected U.S. dollar\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00019",
      "start_char": 3447,
      "end_char": 3666,
      "text": "1 For the latter crisis with exceptional travel restrictions, the reported boom may to some extent reflect the\nreplacement of cash transfers by wire transfers which can be better observed.\n72 INTERNATIONAL MONETARY FUND"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "schema_version": "locator_index_v1",
